 for building programmatic agents with TypeScript. Adds Canvas for interactive visualizations, multitasking with async sub-agents, improved worktrees, and multi-root workspace support.\",\n    33→    \"updateType\": \"feature\",\n    34→    \"url\": \"https://cursor.com/changelog\",\n    35→    \"date\": \"2026-04-29\",\n    36→    \"community_feedback\": []\n    37→  },\n    38→  {\n    39→    \"productName\": \"Claude Code\",\n    40→    \"title\": \"Week 17: Ultrareview Public Preview, Session Recaps, Custom Themes\",\n    41→    \"description\": \"Claude Code Week 17 release introduces /ultrareview public research preview for cloud-based bug-hunting agents, session recaps showing activity while terminal was unfocused, custom themes system, and a redesigned web experience with sessions sidebar and drag-and-drop layout.\",\n    42→    \"updateType\": \"release\",\n    43→    \"url\": \"https://releasebot.io/updates/anthropic/claude-code\",\n    44→    \"date\": \"2026-04-27\",\n    45→    \"community_feedback\": []\n    46→  },\n    47→  {\n    48→    \"productName\": \"Claude Code\",\n    49→    \"title\": \"Week 16: Opus 4.7 Default, xhigh Effort, Cloud Routines, Native CLI Binaries\",\n    50→    \"description\": \"Claude Opus 4.7 becomes default on Max and Team Premium tiers with new 'xhigh' effort level. Also introduces cloud routines for automated agents, /ultrareview parallel code review, /usage tracking, and migration to native CLI binaries for improved performance.\",\n    51→    \"updateType\": \"release\",\n    52→    \"url\": \"https://releasebot.io/updates/anthropic/claude-code\",\n    53→    \"date\": \"2026-04-20\",\n    54→    \"community_feedback\": []\n    55→  }\n    56→]\n    57→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write 